WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our first guest tonight i
a United States Senator

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
from Minnesota.
Her book "Antitrust:

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:05.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
Taking on Monopoly Power
from the Gilded Age

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:07.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the Digital Age"
is available now.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.043 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please welcome back to the sho
Senator Amy Klobuchar.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:11.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much
for being here, Senator.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.146 line:92%
-Hey, Seth, it's great to be on.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, I want to ask, last week,
you were in the room.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:20.387
Obviously, a limited attendanc
for Biden's first big speech

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:21.755 line:92%
Exciting for many people,

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:24.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
especially seeing two wome
on the dais behind him.

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:26.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe a little boring
for other people.

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:29.829 size:88% position:44%
Senators Romney and Cruz
both caught dozing off.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.531 line:92%
How did you find the evening

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:35.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I actually thought
it was a very good speech.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:36.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was such a different
environment,

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:39.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
a little less partisan, becaus
of standing up and down.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I will say, no matter
who the president is,

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:45.545
before you go in there and watch
a long speech at 9:00 -- coffee.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-But you can't drink too much.

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:49.616
You just drink a little.
So that might have helped.

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:51.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think that Joe Biden -
-Yeah, that should be

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:54.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the things they teach
newly elected officials,

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:56.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
insofar as how to deal
with a long speech.

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:58.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-I want to ask about --

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:00.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
Certainly,
in the primary season,

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
both you and President Biden
were framed as moderates

00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:07.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
in, you know, the very crowded
at the time, primary field

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.837 align:start size:94% position:6%
And yet it seems as though -
and certainly laid out

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:13.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
in that speech --
that a very progress agend

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
is being pursued.
How do you feel,

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:18.111 size:78% position:39%
being a part of this,
and do you feel like

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:22.315
that's the way things are going?
-This is the moment

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:23.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
to get things done
for this country.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:27.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think the fact that we'v
had four years of neglect

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
of our democracy
and nothing done

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:33.126 size:88% position:44%
on things like childcare
or really in a big way

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:35.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
on infrastructure,
people want change.

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:39.432
That's why they voted in Georgia
for two Democratic senators.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:43.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's why Joe Biden won b
the big margin that he won by.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so I don't think
it's a surprise

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he's doing well now and
that he's pursuing

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:49.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
this agenda,
and that while we have

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of differences
in our party,

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:53.913 size:81% position:41%
we try to work it out.
And that's how we got

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:56.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
that rescue plan passed.
It was so important,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
as you pointed out
in your openings there,

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:03.756
to get the vaccine numbers up,
to get the economy shored up

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:06.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think
it's what people want.

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's
the right thing to do.

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
And our party,
we may have disagreements --

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:12.499
you see them on the news -
but we don't purge people

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:15.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
and try to kick them out
just because they're

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
telling the truth
like Liz Cheney.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:21.274
-So it's very good timing for us
to have you on,

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.209 align:start size:94% position:6%
talking about
your antitrust book,

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
because so much happened,
even today.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.714 line:92%
For example, Facebook decide

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:31.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
to uphold their ban
on former President Trump,

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:34.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
to extend it
at least another six months.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
What is your reaction
to that news?

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:40.994
-That was the right thing to do.
He's the dis-informer-in-chief

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
He told the biggest lie
of all time,

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:47.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
which led to an insurrection
by getting his followers

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
to believe that the election
was a fake.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.973
It was an outrageous momen
with a number of Republicans

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:55.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
calling him out on it
at the time.

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:59.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, as I said
on that inaugural stage,

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is the time
for our democracy

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:03.516 align:start size:94% position:6%
to pick itself up,
dust itself off, move forward.

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:07.921
And we do not need more
disinformation from Donald Trump

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:11.357
or, for that matter, from others
who are spreading disinformation

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the vaccine,
that's making people think

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:15.528
they're getting a microchi
planted in their shoulder,

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.932
like the guy I met at a café
who said his mother-in-law

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:21.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
wouldn't get it because
she read that on Facebook.

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:24.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
There has to be some rules o
the road in place.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.806 size:75% position:38%
And someone like him
shouldn't be allowed

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:28.975 line:92%
to make people believe lies.

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:30.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, you know, obviously,
this was a decision,

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:33.746
and this was based on rule
that Facebook has laid out

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.648 size:84% position:42%
You know, this was not
government intervention

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:38.284
on the Donald Trump ban,
but I do think it speaks t

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:41.521 size:88% position:44%
how powerful Facebook is
that a former president

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:43.122 size:75% position:38%
is desperate to have
that platform back.

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:47.260
And we saw a lot of members of
the GOP today basically backin

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.729
this idea that you lay out
in your book, as far as,

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:53.333 size:84% position:42%
like, it's important
to break up monopolies.

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:55.301 line:92%
Yet they're not just angry

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the de-platforming
of Donald Trump,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
but they also think
that tech is too "woke."

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:02.041
Do you see this now as a momen
where you might actually hav

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:05.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
some help from across the aisl
in taking steps

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:08.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
to, you know, sort of have
antitrust legislation?

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or do you think
it's just posturing

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:11.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on the fact
that they're angry today

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:12.819 line:92%
about, you know, this decision

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:15.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
that was specific
to the former president?

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There is
some posturing going on.

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:18.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
And this notion they put out
there

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:21.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
that, "Oh, tech is bad
because liberals work there,

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.330
or something like that, that's
not the reason to move forward

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.401
The reason to move forward i
that we have a few companies

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:30.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are the biggest
the world has ever known,

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:32.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are gateways,
and make it really hard

00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:36.709
for competition to come up
they buy everything in sight

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:39.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look at what just --
We had a hearing

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:43.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the app stores, with Google
and Apple in front of us,

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:47.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
and found out from companies
like Spotify, Tile,

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:51.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, yes, match.com
about how they have to pay 30%

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:55.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
of what people buy
on their app store sites,

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:57.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they have to give it
to Apple or Google.

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:59.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
They are told
that they can't even say,

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You can get a better deal
on our website."

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.771
This isn't good for competition.
And we've always been

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:06.205 line:92%
an entrepreneurial country

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
that rejuvenates itself
with capitalism.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
Small businesses emerge.
they do well,

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
they turn sometimes
into big businesses,

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
sometimes not.
But when you have a compan

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:17.850 align:start size:94% position:6%
that buys everything in sigh
and where its head,

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mark Zuckerberg,
literally sent an e-mail

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:21.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
that got discovered last year,
that said,

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:27.460
"We'd rather buy than compete,
that is Exhibit A of a monopoly.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I wanted to make the cas
to the country.

00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:32.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've seen this before.
We did something about it.

00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:35.635 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we need to use our law
to do it again.

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:38.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I think it's interestin
what you just said there,

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.739 line:92%
because, obviously, big business

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:42.408 align:start size:94% position:6%
would frame antitrust
as being anti-capitalist.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:44.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
But can you speak more
to the idea

00:05:44.277 --> 00:05:48.514 align:start size:94% position:6%
that antitrust legislation
can help capitalism

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it evens
the playing field?

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:53.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There's all kinds of examples
of this through history.

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:56.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
Most notably,
the breakup of AT&amp;T.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:58.024 size:78% position:39%
That brought us lower
long-distance rates.

00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:01.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was representing MCI
a bit after this happened,

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:02.428 line:92%
so I know a lot about it.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:05.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
It also started
the cellphone industry up.

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:09.235
Those cellphones used to be th
size of Gordon Gekko's briefcase

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:10.536 line:92%
in the movie "Wall Street.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.438 align:start size:94% position:6%
And now we have
all kinds of options.

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:15.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the former chair of AT&amp;T
himself has said

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:18.378
it made them a stronger compan
because they had to compete.

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:22.148
That's what we need to do again.
It may mean divesting assets

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:25.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
They don't need to own --
Facebook doesn't need to own

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:26.419 size:84% position:42% line:92%
WhatsApp and Instagram.

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe those two companies
would have developed

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:31.824
better privacy policies or
misinformation policies,

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.793 size:81% position:41%
and bells and whistles
we'll never know of.

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:37.997
But they ate them up, becaus
in Mark Zuckerberg's words

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:38.965 size:84% position:42% line:92%
in a discovered e-mail,

00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:42.335 size:78% position:39%
"They could have been
disruptive to us."

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:44.337 line:92%
Competition is about disruption.

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:48.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
I worked in the private sector
I believe in capitalism.

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:50.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I also know
that you have to have

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:52.345 align:start size:94% position:6%
a check and balance
to make sure

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:55.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we keep having new
competitive companies emerge

00:06:55.915 --> 00:07:01.220
-You talk about -- and I don't
think it would be surprising -

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
most people, the first tim
they hear "monopoly"

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:04.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
is because of the board game
Right?

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.126 size:78% position:39%
That's the first time
kids hear that word

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:09.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
and understand a little bi
of what it means.

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:11.964
You lay out the history
that's sort of unknown --

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.366 align:start size:94% position:6%
or not well-known,
I should say --

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:15.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
history of that board game
-Exactly.

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:20.540
And a woman named Lizzie Magie
who is not notable in histor

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.842 align:start size:94% position:6%
and was discovered
through some great reporting

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:25.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
from a "New York Times"-
"Wall Street Journal" reporter

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:27.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
she actually
came up with the idea.

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:29.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
She was strongly against
monopolies.

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:30.950 size:81% position:41% line:92%
Then, what happens is,

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:32.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mr. Darrow and Parker Brothers
come in,

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:35.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
and guess what,
for the first time in history,

00:07:35.922 --> 00:07:38.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
a man got credit
for a woman's idea.

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:40.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-That was it.

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:41.727 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then other men
were like, "That's great.

00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:44.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
We should keep doing that.
-"Let's keep doing it."

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:48.901
-This has been pointed out
by a number of people --

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:52.271
your colleague,
Secretary of Transportation now,

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:54.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
Pete Buttigieg, he had a boo
that just came out

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
called "Trust."
And people were wondering

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:58.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
if "Antitrust" was
a counter to that.

00:07:58.411 --> 00:08:00.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you want to
lay those rumors to rest.

00:08:00.413 --> 00:08:03.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I do.
The rivalry does not continue.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
In fact, I actually
did an interview

00:08:05.785 --> 00:08:09.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
with his book behind me
at one point to promote it

00:08:09.255 --> 00:08:12.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're good friends.
I met his dogs recently.

00:08:12.358 --> 00:08:15.161 align:start size:94% position:6%
We had lunch, went on a walk
He's a great guy.

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:17.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's doing a really good job
But one thing I have

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.733
that Pete doesn't have --
I have over 100 cartoons

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:23.002 size:81% position:41%
in my book, Seth, yes.
-Alright, well, again,

00:08:23.002 --> 00:08:25.705
so if you're a kid at home
looking for the book to buy,

00:08:25.705 --> 00:08:28.541
this one has cartoons and
a backstory about Monopoly

00:08:28.541 --> 00:08:32.078 align:start size:94% position:6%
Speaking about meeting dogs,
have you ever met Major?

00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:33.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
Have you met
the president's dog?

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:36.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-No, I have not.

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:39.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay. Well, maybe.
-I don't know how --

00:08:39.218 --> 00:08:41.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
I heard Major -
didn't he go to Delaware

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:43.189 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be with his family
for a while?

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:45.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah,
that's what they tell you.

00:08:45.591 --> 00:08:47.527 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:08:47.527 --> 00:08:49.228 size:78% position:39%
That's another thing
they tell kids about.

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:51.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
